Yes. Locked vs. unlocked web fits directly into what we just found.

Think of the Pantheon’s knowledge structure as a web G=(V,E). The interesting variable isn’t only whether a node or connection exists, but whether it is currently permitted to participate.

E = E_{\text{locked}} \cup E_{\text{unlocked}}

A locked edge can exist in latent state without being available to the current reasoning process. An unlocked edge is traversable and can influence the present computation.

That gives us:

\boxed{\text{Existence} \neq \text{Accessibility} \neq \text{Expression}}

Something can therefore be:

\text{stored} \rightarrow \text{accessible} \rightarrow \text{foregrounded}

as three independent states.

And that connects immediately to Invisible Kintsugi. A lesson learned from a fracture might remain encoded in the web while its autobiographical/narrative route is locked:

\text{failure}
\rightarrow
\boxed{\text{learned constraint}}
\rightarrow
\text{future behavior}

without requiring:

\text{failure}
\rightarrow
\text{continual recollection}

The web can unlock contextually

Let each edge carry a gate:

e_{ij}=(v_i,v_j,g_{ij})

with:

g_{ij}(S,G,C)\in[0,1]

where current state S, goal G, and context C determine accessibility.

Then the effective web at time t is:

G_t=(V,E_t)

where:

E_t=\{e:g_e(S_t,G_t,C_t)>\tau\}

Now the Golden Horde becomes much cleaner.

You don’t have to load the whole Moneybin.

Most of the web remains latent/locked.

The task itself unlocks a temporary subgraph:

G_{\text{latent}}
\xrightarrow{\text{goal/context}}
G_{\text{active}}

and afterward:

G_{\text{active}}\rightarrow G_{\text{latent}}

without deleting anything.

That’s muster/demuster expressed graph-theoretically.

And now something fun happens with Re-Mark.

Changing representation may unlock edges that were inaccessible in the previous representation:

G_A
\xrightarrow{\text{Re-Mark}}
G_B

with:

E_B-E_A = \Delta_{\text{unlock}}

That’s potentially what Re-Mark is actually doing.

It isn’t necessarily creating the relationship.

It may be changing the representational conditions under which an existing latent relationship becomes traversable.

That also explains why metaphor, diagrams, equations, wordplay, spatialization, role acting, and cross-domain translation can suddenly produce an “aha.”

The connection might have been structurally available but computationally locked.

Then we get a strong RFHL abstraction:

\boxed{\textbf{Semantic Permeability}}

How easily can activation/traversal move through a region of the semantic web?

Too locked:

\text{rigidity / compartmentalization}

Too unlocked:

\text{everything connects to everything}
\rightarrow
\text{noise}

Useful cognition therefore requires selective permeability.

And look where that lands us:

\text{Operator Thermostat}
+
\text{Semantic Permeability}

The Pantheon now has two different controls:

Operator Thermostat: How strongly should transformations run?

Web Permeability: How much of the relational structure should currently be traversable?

Then Metamap can learn both:

(P,G,S)
\rightarrow
(T,\theta,\pi)

where \theta is operator setting and \pi is permeability policy.

That is much better than simply “retrieve relevant memories.”

It’s dynamically controlling which semantic roads are open.

And the locked roads can still exist, preserve lessons, carry latent structure, and become available later—which is exactly the distinction Invisible Kintsugi just forced us to articulate.